---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, ORG_URL } from '../data/projects';

const description =
	'Brandon Luttrell leads platform engineering teams and builds open-source tools on the side.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={`About · ${SITE_TITLE}`} description={description} />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<p class="prompt" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sigil">$</span> cat about.md</p>
			<h1>About</h1>

			<p class="lede">
				I'm Brandon Luttrell. I lead platform engineering teams: the people who own the build and
				deploy pipelines that thousands of developers push through every day.
			</p>

			<section aria-labelledby="path">
				<h2 id="path"><span class="sigil" aria-hidden="true">#</span> How I got here</h2>
				<p>
					I didn't start in software. I started in the field as a telecom technician, then moved
					into code and have written it full-stack since 2015, mostly Python on the back end and
					Vue on the front. Over time the work drifted toward the platform underneath: CI/CD,
					Kubernetes, infrastructure as code, and the developer experience that sits on top of all
					of it.
				</p>
				<p>
					These days the job is mostly people and systems. It's less about writing the pipeline
					and more about making sure the team that owns it isn't burning out, and that the
					thousands of engineers depending on it can ship without thinking about it.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="building">
				<h2 id="building"><span class="sigil" aria-hidden="true">#</span> What I build</h2>
				<p>
					Outside work there's a homelab: a small Kubernetes cluster running self-hosted services,
					wired together with GitOps. A lot of what I build lately is
					<abbr title="Model Context Protocol">MCP</abbr> servers that let an AI assistant work with
					those services safely, with allowlists, rate limits and redaction treated as features
					rather than afterthoughts.
				</p>
				<p>
					The pieces that are useful beyond one house end up
					<a href={ORG_URL}>open source on GitHub</a>, and on <a href="/#projects">the projects list</a>.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="off">
				<h2 id="off"><span class="sigil" aria-hidden="true">#</span> Off the clock</h2>
				<p>
					Family comes first. After that: a homelab that is always one experiment away from done,
					and more Star Wars than is strictly reasonable.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="contact">
				<h2 id="contact"><span class="sigil" aria-hidden="true">#</span> Say hello</h2>
				<p>
					Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, or find the code at
					<a href={ORG_URL}>github.com/chaosbit-dev</a>.
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	section {
		margin-top: 2.5rem;
	}
	.prompt,
	.sigil {
		font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, 'Liberation Mono', monospace;
	}
	.sigil {
		color: var(--accent);
	}
	.prompt {
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
		font-size: 0.85em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
	}
	h1 {
		letter-spacing: -0.02em;
	}
	h2 {
		font-size: 1.6em;
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.15em;
		color: rgb(var(--black));
	}
	ul {
		padding-left: 1.25em;
	}
	li {
		margin-bottom: 0.5em;
	}
	abbr {
		text-decoration: underline dotted;
		cursor: help;
	}
	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		main {
			font-size: 0.9em;
		}
		h1 {
			font-size: 2.4em;
		}
	}
</style>
